# fastlane match — ONE signing certificate, reused, instead of a new one per build.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The lanes used to sign with `-allowProvisioningUpdates`,
# which tells xcodebuild to ask Apple for a distribution certificate whenever it
# cannot find one locally. A CI runner is a fresh machine every time, so it never
# found one, so every build minted another — and Apple caps a standard account at
# three. The account hit that cap twice; the second time it stopped a build the
# day the App Store submission was being prepared. fastlane's own documentation
# names this exact failure and names match as the answer.
#
# WHERE THE CERTIFICATES LIVE. A branch of this repository, carrying nothing but
# match's AES-encrypted files. Two things make that the right call rather than a
# second repository:
#
#   · a second repository cannot be reached by `GITHUB_TOKEN`, so it would need a
#     personal access token, base64-encoded, pasted into a secret — four fiddly
#     steps for the operator instead of one;
#   · GITHUB_TOKEN reaches THIS repository for free, so the only thing anybody has
#     to supply is the passphrase.
#
# The branch carries no code, nothing deploys from it, nothing merges into or out
# of it. It is a store that happens to speak git. See ASSUMPTIONS.md for why that
# is a deliberate exception to "main is the only long-lived branch" rather than a
# reintroduction of the dev branch that rule exists to prevent.
#
# WHAT IS IN IT. Certificates and profiles, encrypted with MATCH_PASSWORD. Anyone
# who can read the branch reads ciphertext; anyone who cannot read the secret has
# nothing.
git_url(ENV["MATCH_GIT_URL"] || "https://github.com/stefangruber001/OpenProject2.git")
git_branch("certificates")
storage_mode("git")

type("appstore")
app_identifier([ENV["APP_BUNDLE_ID"] || "com.caneisubirats.erp"])
team_id(ENV["DEVELOPER_TEAM_ID"] || "5V62K942X6")

# Never create anything by accident. The `certificates` lane turns this off for
# the one run that creates the certificate; every build lane leaves it on, so a
# build can only ever USE what is already stored.
readonly(true)
